1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,600
The Personal Protective Equipment, PPE, worn by actors in this video was selected based on

2
00:00:05,600 --> 00:00:11,360
a risk assessment of the chemicals used in the experiment and conditions simulated in the activity.

3
00:00:11,360 --> 00:00:14,960
In scenes where the presenter did not wear PPE while in the lab,

4
00:00:14,960 --> 00:00:18,000
all chemicals were removed to eliminate chemical hazards.

5
00:00:20,080 --> 00:00:24,400
In the previous video of this series, we looked at general strategies for minimizing risks in the

6
00:00:24,400 --> 00:00:29,200
lab. In this video, we'll learn about some specific techniques for minimizing risk when using

7
00:00:29,200 --> 00:00:33,680
chemicals and lab equipment. Minimizing risk is part of a larger process called RAMP.

8
00:00:34,320 --> 00:00:41,200
RAMP stands for Recognize Hazards, Assess Risks, Minimize Risks, and Prepare for Emergencies.

9
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:45,840
For this video, we'll assume you've already completed the first two steps of RAMP,

10
00:00:45,840 --> 00:00:51,040
Recognize Hazards, and Assess Risks, and also view the previous video about strategies to minimize

11
00:00:51,040 --> 00:00:58,100
risk. There are a wide variety of lab techniques and behaviors that can minimize the risk of

12
00:00:58,100 --> 00:01:02,500
chemical exposure, personal injury, and property damage. Let's look at some examples.

13
00:01:03,460 --> 00:01:07,860
Messy lab spaces increase the risk of trips, spills, or using the wrong reagent during

14
00:01:07,860 --> 00:01:13,060
experimental procedures. Put away book bags and other items unrelated to the experiment.

15
00:01:13,060 --> 00:01:17,380
Also make sure your work area is clean. You should periodically step back and examine your

16
00:01:17,380 --> 00:01:22,340
workspace. Put away unused glassware and equipment and avoid having containers of unneeded

17
00:01:22,340 --> 00:01:27,460
chemicals on the bench. To keep your workspace organized, properly dispose of chemical waste

18
00:01:27,460 --> 00:01:31,300
as soon as it is generated and as directed by your instructor. But make sure you don't

19
00:01:31,300 --> 00:01:36,020
discard chemicals you need to use later. When using a lab notebook, a computer,

20
00:01:36,020 --> 00:01:39,620
phone, or calculator in the lab, be sure to keep them away from chemicals that may

21
00:01:39,620 --> 00:01:43,780
damage or contaminate them. One way to protect calculators and phones is to put them in a

22
00:01:43,780 --> 00:01:48,500
plastic baggie. Now let's look at two common lab techniques and how to reduce the risks

23
00:01:48,500 --> 00:01:56,210
associated with them. Titrations require the use of glassware such as berets, pipettes,

24
00:01:56,210 --> 00:02:00,930
beakers, or flasks. When using glassware, be sure to inspect it for star fractures

25
00:02:02,050 --> 00:02:06,850
or hairline cracks. Simply bumping or heating a flask or beaker with a hairline crack can

26
00:02:06,850 --> 00:02:11,810
cause it to break. Many solutions and solvents look just like water, so it's important to

27
00:02:11,810 --> 00:02:16,850
temporarily label beakers and flasks while in use. Unmarked containers can cause confusion,

28
00:02:16,850 --> 00:02:21,970
which could lead to you or someone else mixing the wrong chemicals. The titrant is a solution

29
00:02:21,970 --> 00:02:26,850
of known concentration added to the burette. Pouring from a reagent bottle can be awkward

30
00:02:26,850 --> 00:02:31,410
and increases the risk of a spill. It's best to pour from a small beaker over which you have

31
00:02:31,410 --> 00:02:37,170
more control. After estimating how much titrant is needed, pour about 10% extra into a labeled

32
00:02:37,170 --> 00:02:41,890
beaker. Be careful not to let drips run down the side of the reagent bottle. This can cause

33
00:02:41,890 --> 00:02:46,530
a minor spill and also leaves drops on the bottle that the next person might touch. Wipe

34
00:02:46,530 --> 00:02:51,650
off any drips and completely replace the lid. When pouring the titrant into the burette,

35
00:02:51,650 --> 00:02:56,690
keep the top of the burette just below eye level. This makes it easier to watch the pouring process.

36
00:02:57,330 --> 00:03:02,130
Be sure to pour slowly, especially if using a funnel, to make sure you don't exceed the volume

37
00:03:02,130 --> 00:03:06,690
of the burette. Place a large beaker under the tip of the burette to catch any spills.

38
00:03:07,810 --> 00:03:11,810
A pipette is often used to measure a known amount of the solution to be analyzed,

39
00:03:11,810 --> 00:03:15,170
also called the analyte. To safely connect the pipette to the bulb,

40
00:03:15,170 --> 00:03:19,010
grasp the pipette close to the end and gently insert it into the bulb.

41
00:03:19,010 --> 00:03:22,690
Applying too much force can break the pipette, which can result in serious injury.

42
00:03:23,570 --> 00:03:28,450
It's important not to place the pipette into the bottle of stock solution to avoid contamination.

43
00:03:28,450 --> 00:03:32,530
Obtain 10% more than you need in your beaker and pipette from the beaker.

44
00:03:32,530 --> 00:03:37,090
Any excess should not go back into the stock solution again to avoid contamination.

45
00:03:37,730 --> 00:03:42,210
If you have leftover titrant or analyte, you can offer to share this with a lab mate,

46
00:03:42,210 --> 00:03:44,530
otherwise dispose of it in the proper waste container.

47
00:03:47,440 --> 00:03:52,480
Chemical synthesis is another common lab experiment with both chemical and procedural hazards.

48
00:03:52,480 --> 00:03:58,080
A common process is to mix solutions of chemicals, heat, and stir the mixture to produce a precipitate,

49
00:03:58,080 --> 00:04:02,960
and isolate the precipitate by filtration. To start a chemical synthesis, you may need to

50
00:04:02,960 --> 00:04:07,360
weigh your reagents. Take your reaction flask and notebook to the balance.

51
00:04:07,360 --> 00:04:12,640
Obtain the desired amount of each solid and small increments. If you get too much at first,

52
00:04:12,640 --> 00:04:17,200
put the excess into a waste container to avoid possible contamination of the original reagent.

53
00:04:18,320 --> 00:04:22,720
If you spill any solid at the balance, clean this up immediately and put the spilled solid

54
00:04:22,720 --> 00:04:28,320
into a waste container. Place the weighed solids into the flask immediately rather than

55
00:04:28,320 --> 00:04:31,360
trying to walk through a crowded lab with the solids on weighing paper.

56
00:04:32,560 --> 00:04:37,760
For reaction in water, heating is usually done on a hot plate. When using any electrical

57
00:04:37,840 --> 00:04:43,040
equipment, it's best to check the cord for frayed connections. Keep the cord off the hot surface and

58
00:04:43,040 --> 00:04:48,560
set the hot plate on a dry surface. If there's any chance a hazardous gas will be produced,

59
00:04:48,560 --> 00:04:53,120
conduct the reaction in a hood where the gas will be quickly removed. This includes reactions that

60
00:04:53,120 --> 00:04:58,000
use a flammable organic solvent, too. A properly functioning hood will minimize the chance that

61
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:03,200
the organic vapor comes in contact with the heat source. Hot glass looks just like cold glass,

62
00:05:03,200 --> 00:05:08,240
and a hot iron ring looks like a cold iron ring. Handle any hot object with beaker tongs or

63
00:05:08,240 --> 00:05:12,800
crucible tongs. These are not interchangeable, and using the wrong tool can lead to dropping

64
00:05:12,800 --> 00:05:18,400
the container, breaking it, or spilling the contents. Sometimes a hot object is best handled

65
00:05:18,400 --> 00:05:23,040
by special heat-resistant gloves. Hot beakers or flasks should only be placed on surfaces

66
00:05:23,040 --> 00:05:28,400
that are heat-resistant. Spills of any solids or liquids should be cleaned up immediately.

67
00:05:28,400 --> 00:05:31,680
Typically, your lab instructor will take care of this or show you how to do so.

68
00:05:34,180 --> 00:05:40,260
As noted earlier, at the end of an experiment, you often have leftover solids, solvents, or solutions.

69
00:05:40,260 --> 00:05:44,900
Share these with lab mates if possible. Somewhere in your lab there will be a waste station that

70
00:05:44,900 --> 00:05:49,620
has containers for the various wastes. As noted earlier, it's very important to put each waste

71
00:05:49,620 --> 00:05:54,580
into a properly labeled container. Many lab mishaps have occurred from mixing incompatible

72
00:05:54,580 --> 00:05:59,620
chemicals, sometimes leading to explosions. Always close the waste container after adding

73
00:05:59,700 --> 00:06:05,220
your solution. When washing your glassware, be sure not to leave anything in the sink.

74
00:06:05,220 --> 00:06:09,140
Broken glassware in the sink is especially hazardous. Be sure to tell your instructor if

75
00:06:09,140 --> 00:06:13,860
you see this. Wipe your area with a wet paper towel or sponge and return all equipment to its

76
00:06:13,860 --> 00:06:18,820
proper location. If you're using a Bunsen burner, double check to make sure that the gas valve

77
00:06:18,820 --> 00:06:23,700
is off. If you use disposable gloves, remove them correctly and discard as directed by your

78
00:06:23,700 --> 00:06:26,980
instructor. And it's always a good idea to wash your hands when you're done.

79
00:06:31,070 --> 00:06:34,830
In this video, we covered some common techniques for minimizing risks in the lab.

80
00:06:35,390 --> 00:06:39,390
The key thing to remember is to be mindful of what you and others are doing in the lab

81
00:06:39,390 --> 00:06:46,350
at all times. And before any experiment, always think about RAMP. Recognize hazards, assess risks,

82
00:06:46,350 --> 00:06:51,070
minimize risks, and prepare for emergencies. To learn more about the other steps in RAMP,

83
00:06:51,070 --> 00:06:55,870
be sure to check out the other five videos in this series. You can find them by clicking here,

84
00:06:55,870 --> 00:07:00,110
checking out the links in the description, or visiting acs.org slash safety videos.

85
00:07:05,330 --> 00:07:11,280
Remove them correctly and discard as acs.org slash videos.
